#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""sync-check -- read-only gauge for the Syncthing cluster and the git roles.

  sync-check sync            ws-1: every folder this machine shares is up to date
  sync-check conflicts       ws-2: Syncthing conflict copies, and stale locks under .git
  sync-check guard           fedxps-3: the reader guard is whole, and no write under
                             a .git this machine only reads, in the disk events or
                             in the version vectors; bigfed-1 on a writer: no git
                             maintenance running or cut short
  sync-check hub             hub-4, hub-1: the hub's free space, pull errors, index
                             and deletion-retention drop-in, over one ssh
  sync-check config          ws-8: stow's links, the lazy lock, claude-link's dry
                             run, bash -n on the shell's files
  sync-check push REPO...    bigfed-2: may this machine push these repositories now
  sync-check                 sync, conflicts, guard, hub, and config without its
                             claude-link dry run (1.8 s), in that order

  -v   every reading under its verdict line      -q   (push) silent for a
  -h   this text                                       repository in no folder

Exit: 0 all clear . 1 something is wrong, or not yet true . 2 could not determine
      . 3 up to date with attention due: DUE lines and nothing worse

The gauge of org/machines/transport-2026-10 (rules.md: ws-1, ws-2, ws-8,
fedxps-3, bigfed-1, bigfed-2, hub-1, hub-4; pilot/build-gauge.md), built
2026-10-10 at the pilot's step 3b and given its hub, config and vector lines,
the second door and the snapshot at step 5a (2026-10-26), in the shape of
disk-check and context-check. Syncthing carries each repository, .git
included, between bigfed and fedxps through nousowl's encrypted hub, and bigfed
alone commits and pushes; this is what says whether a machine may be left, a
repository pushed, or a conflict copy is lying where it loads.

Read-only and unprivileged by construction: no file is written unless the
caller names one in SYNC_CHECK_SNAPSHOT (below), no sudo, and the one request
that is not a GET -- POST /rest/db/scan, which makes Syncthing read the disk so
that a save seconds old is counted -- changes no file. Every git call runs
under GIT_OPTIONAL_LOCKS=0 and is plumbing that writes nothing; claude-link
runs in its dry run, which changes nothing. It binds to Syncthing's documented
REST JSON, to git's plumbing and to systemctl's properties; the two readings
that have no machine-readable form, btrfs's "Free (estimated)" and the
process table's argument lists, are read by their field and by argv words.

Where the readings come from:
  - this machine's Syncthing, at the address its config.xml names, with the API
    key read from that file into a variable and sent as a header, never printed
    and never on a command line; the file is parsed for the fields needed and
    nothing else is extracted, so a folder's encryptionPassword is never held;
  - the hub's views over `ssh -o BatchMode=yes`, by running this same file there
    in its --remote-views mode (python3 reads it from stdin), so the hub's and
    this machine's reads are one implementation; a hub that does not answer is
    a WARN, since nothing can be known without it;
  - every ssh goes through two doors (rules.md -> ws-1, settled 39): the host
    by its device name, the LAN alias, with a 2 s connect timeout, then
    <name>-vpn, the tailnet alias his ~/.ssh/config defines for every
    machine, both inside one wall budget per host (the connect budget plus
    SYNC_CHECK_SSH_GRACE, 30 s), the second door getting what the first left;
    a door that failed to connect or hung is not tried again in the same run,
    and -v names the door that answered;
  - the other workstation's views the same way, only when the hub's view shows
    it owing or needing something, since the hub holds ciphertext and lists
    what a workstation needs under opaque names: the plain names come from that
    workstation alone. A workstation the hub shows disconnected is asleep, and
    what waits for it is not this machine's fault; one the hub shows connected
    but that does not answer leaves the item unverified, a WARN;
  - the version vectors (fedxps-3's durable form, settled 41) from this
    machine's /rest/db/browse and /rest/db/file over two kept-alive
    connections: a counter of this device under a .git it only reads records
    a write since the folder was made, across daemon restarts, and stays in
    the path's vector for good, so a write found and explained is carried by
    its counter in KNOWN_WRITES; the push line reads the same from the
    writer's side, any other device's counter under the repository's .git,
    since a reader's refused checkout or reset moves the index and the working
    tree before its hook refuses and a commit here would carry them. db/browse
    lists no deleted file, so a deletion under .git is seen in the disk events
    alone, and those only since the daemon started;
  - the hub's capacity and its unit from the hub itself, in --remote-hub mode
    (or, in the bare run, beside the sync line's own read of the hub).

Tag semantics, as in disk-check:
  [ OK ]  clear                       [FAIL]  something is wrong
  [WAIT]  not yet: syncing, paused    [DUE]   attention, nothing broken
  [WARN]  could not determine -- never silently assumed healthy
Every line that is not OK prints the paste-ready command or the step that
clears it (rules.md -> him-2 for the stranded cases). A DUE prints only for a
folder otherwise up to date, so a run whose only flags are DUE lines exits 3,
which `bye`, `byebye` and the push gauge in `gpush` pass, the DUE shown
(2026-10-14; org/machines/transport-2026-10/PLAN.md -> settled 37).

Known stuck items are carried by name until repaired, each a FAIL otherwise:
see KNOWN below. SYNC_CHECK_SETTLE (seconds, default 15) is how long a need
may persist with every folder idle before it is called stranded, and
SYNC_CHECK_POLL (default 3) the re-read interval inside that window; the
suite shortens both. SYNC_CHECK_CONFIG names another config.xml (the suite's
fixtures), SYNC_CHECK_SSH_GRACE (seconds, default 30) what a remote command
may take beyond its connect budget. SYNC_CHECK_SNAPSHOT names a file, which
gpushall creates and removes, where a folder's cluster readings are kept for
SYNC_CHECK_SNAPSHOT_TTL seconds (default 300): a later call reads the folder
from there, so a batch reads the cluster once, before its first commit makes
the folder busy, while each repository's own checks still run live; the file
holds statuses, completions, device names and need lists, never a key, and is
written 0600. Suite: tests/sync-check/run.sh.
"""

import glob
import http.client
import json
import os
